    {
      "id": "RuNEn_YNzcqQdEk3tgAy7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 616.7408369785041,
      "y": 38956.597820364696,
      "width": 688.2238901144015,
      "height": 1891.4201293199503,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bSN",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 873109251,
      "version": 232,
      "versionNonce": 1821308899,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1763827488847,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why Both Objects Show the Same Value?\n\nYour code:\n\nStudent stu1 = (Student) context.getBean(\"student1\");\nstu1.setAge(19);\n\nStudent stu2 = (Student) context.getBean(\"student1\");\nSystem.out.println(stu2.getAge());   // prints 19\n\n\nBoth stu1 and stu2 are not separate objects.\n\nThey are references to the same bean instance created by the Spring\nIoC container.\n\n📌 Important: Spring Beans Are Singleton By Default\n\nMeaning:\n\nSpring creates only ONE object of your bean and stores it in the IoC\ncontainer.\nEvery call to getBean(\"student1\") returns the same object, NOT a new\none.\n\nThis is the default behavior of Spring Core.\n\n🔍 Proof with Memory Reference Example\n\nTry printing:\n\nSystem.out.println(stu1);\nSystem.out.println(stu2);\n\n\nYou will get the same memory reference, like:\n\norg.example.Student@4abdb505\norg.example.Student@4abdb505\n\n🧠 Why is Singleton Default?\n\nBecause it:\n\n✔ Saves memory\n✔ Improves performance\n✔ Avoids creating unnecessary objects\n✔ Makes beans reusable\n✔ Keeps DI consistent\n\nSpring assumes most beans are services, not data models.\n\n🔥 How to Make Them Separate Objects Instead? (Prototype Scope)\n\nIf you want new, separate objects each time, use:\n\n@Scope(\"prototype\")\n@Component\npublic class Student {\n}\n\n\nOr in XML:\n\n<bean id=\"student1\" class=\"org.example.Student\" scope=\"prototype\"/>\n\n\nNow:\n\nStudent stu1 = context.getBean(\"student1\");\nstu1.setAge(19);\n\nStudent stu2 = context.getBean(\"student1\");\nSystem.out.println(stu2.getAge());  // will not be 19\n\n\n✔ Because Spring now creates a new object for every getBean() call.\n\n🎯 Conclusion\nScope                      Behavior\nsingleton (default)        One object for whole app → same reference\nprototype                  New object for each getBean()\n\nYour case = singleton, so value modification appears on both\nreferences.",
      "fontSize": 17.801601217128944,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why Both Objects Show the Same Value?\n\nYour code:\n\nStudent stu1 = (Student) context.getBean(\"student1\");\nstu1.setAge(19);\n\nStudent stu2 = (Student) context.getBean(\"student1\");\nSystem.out.println(stu2.getAge());   // prints 19\n\n\nBoth stu1 and stu2 are not separate objects.\n\nThey are references to the same bean instance created by the Spring IoC container.\n\n📌 Important: Spring Beans Are Singleton By Default\n\nMeaning:\n\nSpring creates only ONE object of your bean and stores it in the IoC container.\nEvery call to getBean(\"student1\") returns the same object, NOT a new one.\n\nThis is the default behavior of Spring Core.\n\n🔍 Proof with Memory Reference Example\n\nTry printing:\n\nSystem.out.println(stu1);\nSystem.out.println(stu2);\n\n\nYou will get the same memory reference, like:\n\norg.example.Student@4abdb505\norg.example.Student@4abdb505\n\n🧠 Why is Singleton Default?\n\nBecause it:\n\n✔ Saves memory\n✔ Improves performance\n✔ Avoids creating unnecessary objects\n✔ Makes beans reusable\n✔ Keeps DI consistent\n\nSpring assumes most beans are services, not data models.\n\n🔥 How to Make Them Separate Objects Instead? (Prototype Scope)\n\nIf you want new, separate objects each time, use:\n\n@Scope(\"prototype\")\n@Component\npublic class Student {\n}\n\n\nOr in XML:\n\n<bean id=\"student1\" class=\"org.example.Student\" scope=\"prototype\"/>\n\n\nNow:\n\nStudent stu1 = context.getBean(\"student1\");\nstu1.setAge(19);\n\nStudent stu2 = context.getBean(\"student1\");\nSystem.out.println(stu2.getAge());  // will not be 19\n\n\n✔ Because Spring now creates a new object for every getBean() call.\n\n🎯 Conclusion\nScope                      Behavior\nsingleton (default)        One object for whole app → same reference\nprototype                  New object for each getBean()\n\nYour case = singleton, so value modification appears on both references.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "e34EcMwrGKYNB-X2oZlhx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 682.3183848445094,
      "y": 41204.13203338464,
      "width": 849.7908080083741,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bSO",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 175981293,
      "version": 77,
      "versionNonce": 722776995,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1763828261763,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "04 - Scopes \nIn Spring, beans can have different scopes that define how many instances of\nthe \nbean are created and how they are shared across the application.  \n\nTypes of Bean Scopes: \n\n1. Singleton (Default Scope): \n○ In the Singleton scope, only one instance of the bean is created, even \nif there are multiple references to it.\n  \n○ Every time we retrieve the bean from the Spring container, we will get \nthe same instance. \n\n2. Prototype: \n○ In the Prototype scope, a new object is created each time when we call \nthe getBean() method.\n \n○ Each reference will point to a distinct instance of the bean. \n\n3. Request (for Spring Web): \n○ In the Request scope, a new bean instance is created for every HTTP \nrequest. This scope is available in web applications. \n\n4. Session (for Spring Web): \n○ In the Session scope, a new bean instance is created for every HTTP \nsession. This scope is specific to web applications.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "04 - Scopes \nIn Spring, beans can have different scopes that define how many instances of the \nbean are created and how they are shared across the application.  \n\nTypes of Bean Scopes: \n\n1. Singleton (Default Scope): \n○ In the Singleton scope, only one instance of the bean is created, even \nif there are multiple references to it.\n  \n○ Every time we retrieve the bean from the Spring container, we will get \nthe same instance. \n\n2. Prototype: \n○ In the Prototype scope, a new object is created each time when we call \nthe getBean() method.\n \n○ Each reference will point to a distinct instance of the bean. \n\n3. Request (for Spring Web): \n○ In the Request scope, a new bean instance is created for every HTTP \nrequest. This scope is available in web applications. \n\n4. Session (for Spring Web): \n○ In the Session scope, a new bean instance is created for every HTTP \nsession. This scope is specific to web applications.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "DpwqITAj5igKgCdCxuEb1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 687.9668679186987,
      "y": 41939.460996903086,
      "width": 720.4609375,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
