      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "5k9xBN5tt1V_wh_XPsBV9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 569.7304213171474,
      "y": 56808.834456990175,
      "width": 880.6383076382318,
      "height": 1225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bTY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1715323949,
      "version": 57,
      "versionNonce": 218111939,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1763881009321,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. What is Loose Coupling? (GOOD)\n\nTwo classes are loosely coupled when:\n\n✔ They depend on abstractions/interfaces, not concrete classes\n✔ One class does NOT create its own dependencies\n✔ You can replace the dependency without modifying the class\n✔ More flexible\n✔ Easy to test\n✔ Dependency Injection becomes possible\n\n✅ Example: Loose Coupling (using interface + DI)\ninterface Vehicle {\n    void start();\n}\n\nclass Car implements Vehicle {\n    public void start() { System.out.println(\"Car started\"); }\n}\n\nclass ElectricCar implements Vehicle {\n    public void start() { System.out.println(\"Electric car started\"); }\n}\n\nclass Driver {\n    private Vehicle vehicle;\n\n    public Driver(Vehicle vehicle) { // Inject dependency\n        this.vehicle = vehicle;\n    }\n\n    void drive() {\n        vehicle.start();\n    }\n}\n\nWhy is this loose coupling?\n\nDriver depends on Vehicle interface, not Car\n\nReplace Car with ElectricCar without changing Driver\n\nDriver d = new Driver(new ElectricCar());\n\n\n✔ Easy to expand\n✔ Easy to test\n✔ Flexible\n✔ Clean OOP",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. What is Loose Coupling? (GOOD)\n\nTwo classes are loosely coupled when:\n\n✔ They depend on abstractions/interfaces, not concrete classes\n✔ One class does NOT create its own dependencies\n✔ You can replace the dependency without modifying the class\n✔ More flexible\n✔ Easy to test\n✔ Dependency Injection becomes possible\n\n✅ Example: Loose Coupling (using interface + DI)\ninterface Vehicle {\n    void start();\n}\n\nclass Car implements Vehicle {\n    public void start() { System.out.println(\"Car started\"); }\n}\n\nclass ElectricCar implements Vehicle {\n    public void start() { System.out.println(\"Electric car started\"); }\n}\n\nclass Driver {\n    private Vehicle vehicle;\n\n    public Driver(Vehicle vehicle) { // Inject dependency\n        this.vehicle = vehicle;\n    }\n\n    void drive() {\n        vehicle.start();\n    }\n}\n\nWhy is this loose coupling?\n\nDriver depends on Vehicle interface, not Car\n\nReplace Car with ElectricCar without changing Driver\n\nDriver d = new Driver(new ElectricCar());\n\n\n✔ Easy to expand\n✔ Easy to test\n✔ Flexible\n✔ Clean OOP",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
