    {
      "id": "oyykveY3Vy4kpsUEHr0ys",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 572.8098917093905,
      "y": 62629.85082063347,
      "width": 943.5812036634615,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bU1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2110308365,
      "version": 72,
      "versionNonce": 1579255875,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1763881232124,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "08 - Creating Interface \nIn Spring, interfaces are often used to achieve loose coupling between components, \nallowing for flexible design and easy substitution of implementations.  \n● An interface defines a set of methods that implementing classes must \nprovide. \n● Spring can inject different implementations of the same interface based on \nthe configuration. \n● Different classes can implement the same interface. \n● We can switch between implementations by specifying which one to inject \nin the Spring configuration.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "08 - Creating Interface \nIn Spring, interfaces are often used to achieve loose coupling between components, \nallowing for flexible design and easy substitution of implementations.  \n● An interface defines a set of methods that implementing classes must \nprovide. \n● Spring can inject different implementations of the same interface based on \nthe configuration. \n● Different classes can implement the same interface. \n● We can switch between implementations by specifying which one to inject \nin the Spring configuration.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
